{"source": "inputs/prior/research/research/proposal.md", "source_sha256": "b05f026a87e73b223788f4c0a71d1339bf22b0dfe7a6dde045301db52e3d0dde", "lines": [{"line": 1, "text": "# Biomedical image review workspace: research proposal"}, {"line": 2, "text": ""}, {"line": 3, "text": "**Stage:** brief-only research proposal, 5 October 2026  "}, {"line": 4, "text": "**Scope:** a small desktop research tool for six researchers exchanging projects through shared storage. This proposal is not a clinical product specification and does not claim that an existing component meets the performance target."}, {"line": 5, "text": ""}, {"line": 6, "text": "## Recommendation"}, {"line": 7, "text": ""}, {"line": 8, "text": "Build a desktop shell that owns the project, import decisions, coordinate contract, provenance, recovery, and exports. Use **OME-Zarr 0.5.2** as the first chunked interchange/storage path; add a deliberately bounded **single-file OME-TIFF/TIFF** read path using `tifffile==2026.9.20` and the documented `zarr==3.4.0` pairing as an integration-spike pin. Treat these as candidate pins, not a validated stack. Use a napari canvas/annotation layer only if a short compatibility spike identifies and pins a maintained release that accepts the selected lazy arrays; napari 0.4.19 is a historical mechanism precedent, not a proposed deployment version. Keep a native canvas as a bounded fallback."}, {"line": 9, "text": ""}, {"line": 10, "text": "The application owns a versioned project manifest and journal. It references immutable source bytes by content identity, retains raw metadata, expresses coordinates and transformations explicitly, and writes annotations and derived data as separate assets. It never writes into original source images. View settings are display state; resampling is a provenance-bearing data operation."}, {"line": 11, "text": ""}, {"line": 12, "text": "## Source-backed mechanisms and what they establish"}, {"line": 13, "text": ""}, {"line": 14, "text": "**Fact — coordinate and multiscale representation.** OME-NGFF 0.5.2 describes Zarr v3 images with named dimensions, axis types/units, and scale/translation coordinate transformations. It requires dimension names to match the array dimensions. For multiscales, only scale and translation are permitted, with a single scale and optional translation per level; spatial axes have specified ordering guidance. It therefore supplies a useful interoperable model for common axis-aligned images, but it does not express every affine orientation the application may encounter. Its labels convention describes integer-valued label images and a link to the source image. [S1, §§1.1, 2.1, 2.3–2.4, 2.6]"}, {"line": 15, "text": ""}, {"line": 16, "text": "**Fact — TIFF plane mapping is metadata-dependent.** OME-TIFF stores OME-XML in the first TIFF IFD's `ImageDescription`; `DimensionOrder` and `TiffData` map IFDs to Z/C/T planes. Multi-file datasets use UUID references. A TIFF file's page order alone is not a safe axis interpretation. [S2, OME-XML metadata, `DimensionOrder`, `TiffData`, Multi-file OME-TIFF]"}, {"line": 17, "text": ""}, {"line": 18, "text": "**Independent implementation precedent — tifffile.** The tifffile 2026.9.20 documentation describes access to TIFF strips, tiles, pages, SubIFDs, series, and pyramid levels through array/Zarr-oriented interfaces. It documents both supported formats and meaningful limitations, including incomplete/uneven support for some vendor formats. That makes it a plausible bounded TIFF reader and a useful example of format-specific handling. It does not show that this application can meet its memory, latency, cancellation, or project-recovery targets. The captured docs were truncated at 512 KiB, so only the captured overview, requirements, notes, and `TiffPageSeries` API material are relied on here. The docs list `zarr==3.4.0` and `imagecodecs==2026.8.16`; tifffile's revision notes say `return_as` was added and `aszarr` deprecated in 2026.5.2. Pin and test the current API rather than copying an older example. [S3, Overview, Requirements, Notes, revisions, `TiffPageSeries`]"}, {"line": 19, "text": ""}, {"line": 20, "text": "**Independent implementation precedent and consequential failure chain — napari labels.** napari issue #6079 reports that painting Zarr/TensorStore-backed labels did not update the visible display. The issue's technical discussion attributes the stale view to sliced data being a copy for those backends, while NumPy slices were views. Commit `658b080d62bd9c6f478f781163020cdcf5a44139` (25 August 2023; PR #6112) changes the labels update path to update the visible slice for non-NumPy-backed data and adds rendering regression tests covering canvas updates after paint, fill, and mouse edits across NumPy, Zarr, and TensorStore cases. The code and those tests are present at napari tag `v0.4.19`; the sampled `v0.4.18` implementation lacks that direct visible-slice update. This bounds release applicability to the two inspected tags; it does not establish the first fixed release or that the suite was executed here. The tests exercise small arrays and rendering, not file-backed 3 GB sources, restart, durable undo, atomic saves, or coordinate round trips. The practical lesson is to test rendered state against authoritative backing data for every storage backend, including edits, and not infer correctness from a successful write. [S4, issue #6079; S5, commit and diff; S6–S8, `v0.4.18`/`v0.4.19` labels implementation and tests]"}, {"line": 21, "text": ""}, {"line": 22, "text": "Napari's shapes code also provides a useful interaction precedent: vertices, shape types, per-shape features/styles, and spatial transforms are explicit concepts. Its layer source is not evidence of project persistence or interoperability. [S9, napari `v0.4.19` Shapes source]"}, {"line": 23, "text": ""}, {"line": 24, "text": "## Proposed architecture and support boundary"}, {"line": 25, "text": ""}, {"line": 26, "text": "1. **Desktop shell and project service.** A Qt desktop shell owns project lifecycle, keyboard commands, status/progress, metadata inspection, style library, and export. Napari may host the canvas/layers after a versioned integration spike; it does not own the authoritative project or coordinate model. Choose a maintained GUI/backend combination and lockfile only after checking actual napari, Qt, Python, NumPy, Zarr, and reader compatibility. The current napari stable documentation describes higher-dimensional viewing and points/shapes/labels, but the exact runtime release to integrate remains unresolved. [S10]"}, {"line": 27, "text": "2. **Reader adapters.** Initial support: OME-Zarr 0.5.2 datasets that meet the app's axis, chunk, and transform rules; and single-file OME-TIFF/TIFF series where the OME-XML/page mapping is unambiguous, the required codecs are available, and the selected access path is proven bounded. Treat BigTIFF as eligible only after it passes the same tests. Do not promise universal TIFF compatibility. Defer multi-file OME-TIFF, proprietary CZI/LIF/ND2 and microscopy-specific edge cases pending representative data and adapters. Unsupported compression, malformed metadata, missing codec, ambiguous series/axes, or a decode unit exceeding the configured limit must stop before image registration, explain the reason, and leave the project and source intact. Offer metadata-only inspection and an explicit import correction when feasible."}, {"line": 28, "text": "3. **Project assets.** A project directory contains a versioned JSON manifest, raw metadata captures, annotation records, chunked integer mask assets, operation journal, and optional derived images. Store multi-point geometry and provenance in the project schema; optionally mirror mask arrays into NGFF `labels` for interchange. Do not make the NGFF labels metadata the sole record of points, styles, undo history, or app-specific scope."}, {"line": 29, "text": ""}, {"line": 30, "text": "This boundary deliberately favors formats with inspectable axes and chunk/plane access. Bio-Formats 8.5.0 is an opportunity for a later adapter/conversion path: its own format matrix reports more than 140 formats but indicates read/write direction and support strength per format, so that count is not a compatibility promise. [S11]"}, {"line": 31, "text": ""}, {"line": 32, "text": "## Data contract and workflow"}, {"line": 33, "text": ""}, {"line": 34, "text": "### Identity, axes, calibration, and provenance"}, {"line": 35, "text": ""}, {"line": 36, "text": "Each image asset records a stable project UUID; source URI and relative path when available; original basename; byte length; instrument/series identifiers; a streaming SHA-256 once complete; file size and modification time as relocation hints; source sample type/endianness; shape; native axis names/order; raw metadata bytes plus digest; parser/version; parsed metadata; and any user correction as a separate override with actor/time/reason. Path, size, and mtime are hints, not identity. Hashing is cancellable and bounded-memory; until it completes, label identity provisional. On relocation, match by hash, then show a conflict/relink choice if bytes differ; never silently accept a same-name replacement."}, {"line": 37, "text": ""}, {"line": 38, "text": "Keep native dimension order as read and store an explicit mapping to canonical named axes (`t`, `c`, `z`, `y`, `x`). Never squeeze singleton axes or guess an unknown axis. If an axis or calibration is ambiguous, show raw metadata beside the proposed interpretation. Let the user accept an explicit mapping/calibration override while retaining the untouched source metadata; otherwise allow metadata inspection only and mark the image unsupported for annotation."}, {"line": 39, "text": ""}, {"line": 40, "text": "**Product choice, to validate:** integer voxel indices are zero-based and denote sample centers. Store `index_axes`, `physical_axes`, units, and a full double-precision homogeneous affine. Define column-vector mapping `physical = A × [index, 1]`; matrix columns correspond exactly to `index_axes`, rows to `physical_axes`, and the last column is origin. For a `zyx` array with spacing `[2, 0.5, 0.25]` and physical XYZ origin `[10,20,30]`, index `[2,3,4]` maps to XYZ `[11,21.5,34]`. This explicit convention avoids confusing storage order with world order. Persist full affine orientation even when the source standard cannot represent it. Write OME-NGFF transforms only when the transform is representable by its scale/translation model; otherwise preserve the authoritative matrix in the project sidecar and state the limitation in export metadata."}, {"line": 41, "text": ""}, {"line": 42, "text": "A geometric transform or resampling operation creates a new image asset ID and records parent image ID/hash, exact matrix, output axes/shape/dtype, interpolation, boundary rules, software version, and timestamp. Original pixels remain unchanged. An annotation remains bound to its original coordinate-frame ID; moving it into a derived frame is an explicit recorded transform. Display projection, slice, zoom, channel selection, LUT and window/level are saved as view state, not as new image samples."}, {"line": 43, "text": ""}, {"line": 44, "text": "### Annotations and interaction"}, {"line": 45, "text": ""}, {"line": 46, "text": "An annotation record has ID, image/source hash, geometry kind, coordinate-frame ID, scope, fixed-axis selectors, geometry payload, style ID/version, author, timestamps, and edit revision. Point and polygon coordinates use the declared voxel-index axes and may include computed physical coordinates; the index coordinates and frame are authoritative. A plane annotation records its fixed z/t/c values as applicable. A volume annotation has no fixed spatial slice; acquisition-wide scope is distinct from its geometry. A mask is a chunked integer array whose asset and label table are versioned and checksummed. Do not encode a point/polygon as a painted mask without retaining its original geometry."}, {"line": 47, "text": ""}, {"line": 48, "text": "Provide project-level reusable styles for points, polygon outlines/fills, and mask labels. Make selection, drawing, deletion, channel/time/slice navigation, save, undo and redo keyboard-operable, with visible focus and legible non-color-only metadata/error/status feedback. Group a continuous stroke into a coherent undo command. Persist edits as a bounded command/tile-patch journal with periodic checkpoints; cap memory and disk history and expose the history policy. napari 0.4.19 demonstrates in-memory label history, but the source checked does not establish durable cross-restart history; persistence is an app responsibility. [S7, labels history implementation]"}, {"line": 49, "text": ""}, {"line": 50, "text": "### Save, reopen, and shared storage"}, {"line": 51, "text": ""}, {"line": 52, "text": "Import never rewrites the original. Default to external read-only references for multi-gigabyte sources; offer copy-into-project as a deliberate portability choice, with a streaming progress display. A portable bundle is self-contained only if it includes every referenced source (or a verified project-owned copy), metadata, masks, annotations, and derived assets. Otherwise show which assets remain external and how to relocate them."}, {"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "For each save, write changed assets into a same-volume staging generation, verify lengths/checksums and manifest references, flush where supported, then publish a small `CURRENT` pointer with atomic replace; retain the last known-good generation until the new one validates. On startup, validate `CURRENT`, scan staging/previous generations for a complete recovery point, and offer recovery with a clear timestamp. Import enters the manifest only after its metadata and required identity checks finish. Exports use temporary outputs and publish by rename after validation. This is a design proposal: rename/flush guarantees differ across filesystems, particularly shared/network storage, and must be tested on the group's actual share. Do not claim a transaction on SMB/NFS based solely on local-filesystem behavior."}, {"line": 55, "text": ""}, {"line": 56, "text": "With six independent workstations and ordinary shared storage, use a simple single-writer lock/revision check. Detect stale locks and revision conflicts; preserve both recoverable copies and offer reload/save-copy rather than silently overwriting. This supports bundle exchange, not live collaboration or conflict-free merge."}, {"line": 57, "text": ""}, {"line": 58, "text": "### Bounded access and responsiveness"}, {"line": 59, "text": ""}, {"line": 60, "text": "A 3 GB source must never require a second full decoded image in RAM. Open by metadata and tiles/chunks/planes, use a byte-budgeted LRU cache (initial proposal: 512 MiB), limit in-flight decode and prefetch, and ensure each supported codec/access path can stop at a tile/page/chunk boundary. Keep decoding off the UI thread. Report bytes/planes/chunks, progress, errors, and cancellation; discard incomplete staging assets. If a compressed strip or other indivisible decode unit is too large to meet the measured memory budget, refuse that import path with a clear reason or require an explicit conversion workflow. Do not transparently materialize the whole dataset."}, {"line": 61, "text": ""}, {"line": 62, "text": "**Critical assumption:** the documented tifffile/Zarr APIs and the eventual GUI canvas can deliver random slice/tile access with bounded decode and useful cancellation for representative real 3 GB files. None of the sources or this proposal establishes that. Verify peak process memory, cache bound, first-view latency, pan/slice/channel latency, edit latency, cancellation delay, and reopen time on the ordinary 8-core/16 GB target, with and without optional GPU. Candidate thresholds for the first spike: 512 MiB cache; no more than one decode unit per worker; acknowledge cancellation within one second at a chunk boundary. These are proposed acceptance criteria, not measured results."}, {"line": 63, "text": ""}, {"line": 64, "text": "## Exports and honesty about transformations"}, {"line": 65, "text": ""}, {"line": 66, "text": "- **Annotation table:** CSV with image/project IDs, source SHA-256 (or provisional marker), series, geometry kind, coordinate frame, index-axis names and zero-based coordinates, physical coordinates/units when calibrated, scope/fixed-axis values, style and revision. Store polygon vertex lists as a documented JSON cell or a normalized companion table. For masks, include asset path, label IDs, shape/axes/transform, and checksum. State that CSV alone does not preserve raster mask pixels or all style/project history."}, {"line": 67, "text": "- **Overlay:** export a selected 2D plane with its t/z/channel(s), source identity, window/level/LUT, color/opacity, display transform, and annotation coordinates in a sidecar. A rendered RGB/PNG overlay bakes display mapping and appearance and is not the original sample array. If exporting a pixel mask, preserve integer label values and supply axis/transform metadata; never imply that a flat image carries all annotation scope."}, {"line": 68, "text": "- **Small derived image:** export an explicitly selected crop/plane and record source ID/hash, axes, index bounds, dtype, whether any resampling occurred, transform/interpolation, and output hash. A crop without resampling may preserve source samples and dtype; a rendered conversion or resample changes values and must be described. Show a before/after summary and source hash verification so users can check what changed and what stayed identical."}, {"line": 69, "text": ""}, {"line": 70, "text": "## Useful opportunity and alternative"}, {"line": 71, "text": ""}, {"line": 72, "text": "**Opportunity:** use OME-Zarr multiscales and `labels` as a standards-based route to chunked image/mask interchange with named axes and calibration. This could reduce repeated conversion and aid project-bundle exchange. Test interoperability with at least two independent readers and confirm the selected Zarr v3 implementation writes metadata those readers interpret as intended. [S1]"}, {"line": 73, "text": ""}, {"line": 74, "text": "**Alternative:** for vendor inputs outside the initial boundary, evaluate Bio-Formats or a controlled Bio-Formats-to-OME-Zarr conversion step rather than writing an unmaintainable parser for every instrument. Its per-format support matrix makes a declared, tested allowlist essential. Defer live cloud sync, multiuser merge, automatic segmentation, and registration; preserve leads for them, but they do not solve the first-order identity, axes, recovery, and export contract. [S11]"}, {"line": 75, "text": ""}, {"line": 76, "text": "## Proposed validation, not yet performed"}, {"line": 77, "text": ""}, {"line": 78, "text": "1. Build a version-locked spike for current napari/Qt/Zarr/tifffile and representative OME-Zarr plus TIFF inputs. Confirm lazy access path, edit refresh for every backend, codecs, restart, and supported OSs. Napari's historical fix is a regression-test pattern, not a substitute."}, {"line": 79, "text": "2. Create a synthetic 5D `t,c,z,y,x` image with non-unit anisotropic spacing, nonzero origin, known axis permutation, and known channel/slice. Verify index↔physical round trips, named-axis selection, scale/translation export, unsupported rotation behavior, and that display window/level leaves source bytes/hash unchanged."}, {"line": 80, "text": "3. Exercise a representative 3 GB compressed and uncompressed source on 8-core/16 GB hardware. Measure peak RSS, cache size, UI responsiveness, cancellation, and restart from interrupted import/save/export. Reject formats whose largest decode unit violates the budget."}, {"line": 81, "text": "4. Inject termination between staged mask chunks, manifest write, `CURRENT` replace, and export rename; reopen and verify either the previous complete generation or the new complete generation is available. Repeat on the actual shared-storage filesystem."}, {"line": 82, "text": "5. Export point/polygon/mask, CSV, overlay, and small derived image; reopen on a second workstation and compare source hashes, geometry/frame metadata, styles, display state, mask checksums, and exact written sample values."}, {"line": 83, "text": "6. Keep an allowlist with instrument, firmware/software version, sample file identity, tested series/axes/codecs, read/write behavior, and known limitations. A newly observed format/version does not become supported until its test fixture passes."}, {"line": 84, "text": ""}, {"line": 85, "text": "## Candidate witness executed in this stage"}, {"line": 86, "text": ""}, {"line": 87, "text": "An isolated Python arithmetic check (receipt `exec-tukp86p1`) evaluated the declared `zyx` index-to-XYZ example and an 8-bit display mapping for samples `[0,1000,65535]` under window `[0,65535]`. Expected output was physical `[11,21.5,34]`, display `[0,4,255]`, and unchanged input samples. It exited 0 and printed those values. **Scope:** this checks only candidate-authored arithmetic and a pure display mapping; it does not test any viewer, file reader, metadata parser, save/export code, or application behavior. All system-level validation above remains proposed/UNEXECUTED."}, {"line": 88, "text": ""}, {"line": 89, "text": "## Discovery method and limits"}, {"line": 90, "text": ""}, {"line": 91, "text": "Roughly 30% of planned discovery was reserved prospectively for mechanism families outside whole-product viewer comparisons: storage/axis standards (OME-NGFF), TIFF plane mapping and chunk/page reading (OME-TIFF/tifffile), and a format-bridge opportunity (Bio-Formats). No elapsed-time measurements were recorded, and this is not a claim that all candidate leads were verified. The central lessons that change the plan are to keep complete coordinate transforms app-owned when standards cannot express them, gate imports on explicit plane/axis mapping, and test view refresh against backing storage for non-NumPy edits. Captured source identities and applicability are cataloged in `sources.json`; optional and unresolved leads are in `leads.json`."}]}
